From b0e7f07b04f20f65f499879de118b0433a6afd05 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 05:43:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] expand: a struct, enum or union body's trailing comma goes to
 a proc macro as written

riff 3.7.0, git-version 0.3.4 lib.rs:51 `#[proc_macro_hack] pub use
git_version_macro::git_version;`: `error:0: compile_error! "expected `,`"`.

proc-macro-hack 0.5.19 wraps its expansion in `enum Dummy { Value =
(stringify! { .. }, 0).1, }` and hands that to its own `ProcMacroHack`
derive, whose parse_enum_hack wants the `,` after the variant. Since 0451
(C300) the visitor wrote commas only between list entries, so every plain
`#[proc_macro_hack] pub use` re-export failed (phf 0.8, hex-literal 0.2,
indoc 0.3, paste 0.1, git-version 0.3). rustc hands over the tokens as
written.

The parser now records whether a struct, tuple struct, enum, enum variant
field list or union body ended with a comma (AST m_trailing_comma, kept
by clone()), and the visitor writes it. The pre-derive cfg filter sets it
when it drops the last entries: rustc captures an entry together with its
own comma, so the last one kept keeps the comma that followed it.

The expression printer (ast/dump.cpp, re-lexed as proc-macro input for
discriminants, field defaults and attribute-macro fn bodies) wrote a comma
after every tuple and array element; now only between entries, as C300
did for items, keeping `(a,)`. proc-macro-hack's `(stringify! { .. }, 0)`
had gone out as `( .. , 0 , )`.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
